## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to reason about lightweight on-device features under real-world constraints, including energy budgets, privacy regulations, and user consent requirements.

## Key Concepts
- Cost-benefit analysis under resource constraints
- On-device caching strategies and cache invalidation
- Energy-aware prefetching policies
- Privacy compliance and consent overhead
- Phased rollout strategies and success metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
const PREFETCH_CONFIG = {
  batteryThreshold: 0.25,
  network: 'wifi',
  cacheBudgetMB: 75,
  maxMemoryPercent: 15,
  retryIntervalMs: 30 * 60 * 1000
};

class AssetPrefetcher {
  constructor(config = PREFETCH_CONFIG) {
    this.config = config;
    this.cache = new Map();
  }

  async shouldPrefetch(deviceState) {
    const { batteryLevel, networkType, isCharging } = deviceState;
    return (
      batteryLevel > this.config.batteryThreshold &&
      (networkType === 'wifi' || isCharging)
    );
  }

  async prefetch(assets) {
    if (!(await this.shouldPrefetch(getDeviceState()))) return;
    for (const asset of assets) {
      const cached = await caches.match(asset.url);
      if (!cached) {
        const response = await fetch(asset.url);
        await caches.put(asset.url, response.clone());
      }
    }
  }

  async getAsset(url) {
    const cached = await caches.match(url);
    if (cached) return cached;
    return this.handleMiss(url);
  }

  async handleMiss(url) {
    return fetch(url).catch(() => getOfflineFallback(url));
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a practical on-device data structure with incremental updates, offline-first UX, and a clear 12‑month cost plan, including privacy considerations.

## Key Concepts

- Local inverted index and incremental updates
- Offline-first data paths and cache invalidation
- Delta-based cloud sync and conflict resolution
- Resource budgeting: storage, CPU, memory, latency, energy
- Privacy, compliance, and user consent handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal incremental inverted index (conceptual)
class InvertedIndex {
  constructor() {
    this.index = {}; // term -> Set of note IDs
  }
  addNote(id, text) {
    text.split(/\W+/).forEach(t => {
      if (!t) return;
      const w = t.toLowerCase();
      (this.index[w] ||= new Set()).add(id);
    });
  }
  search(q) {
    return q
      .toLowerCase()
      .split(/\s+/)
      .flatMap(w => Array.from(this.index[w] || []));
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you estimate index maintenance energy and storage growth over time?
- How would you handle search latency guarantees on mid-range devices with thousands of notes?
- What privacy safeguards would you implement for on-device indexing and sync deltas?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical on-device crypto trade-offs for privacy-focused apps.

## Key Concepts
- On-device key management
- Key rotation cadence
- AES-256-GCM encryption
- Impact on battery, CPU, memory
- Privacy/compliance costs

## Code Example
```javascript
async function encryptNote(note, key) {
  const enc = new TextEncoder();
  const iv = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(12));
  const alg = { name: 'AES-GCM', iv };
  const ciphertext = await crypto.subtle.encrypt(alg, key, enc.encode(note));
  return { iv, ciphertext: new Uint8Array(ciphertext) };
}
```

